	)
	(text "Note that the BMI160 is pin compatible with\nthe BMI270, which means you can use the same \nfootprint and the same symbols for both. This\nis why the symbol shown here is labeled \n\"BMI160\"; to avoid having to draw our own \nsymbol we instead just choose a compatible one."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 129.54 48.26 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "2e1735dd-321e-4d75-8215-42ee471614ef")
	)
	(text "Often, you won't need to use some \npins. If this is the case, check\nif the modules wants you to leave\nit unconnected, grounded, or flagged\nas no-connect. Refer to a datasheet!\nFor the BMI260, ASDx and ASCx are for \nauxiliary sensors, used to bring the \n6-axis IMU up to 9-axis (For certain\napplications). For simplicity, we will\nnot use it here. OCSB and OSDO refer to\npins meant for a device specific function,\nit seems to be Bosch-specific and we don't\nreally need it. Its function is probably\nirrelevant to our aerospace applications."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 160.782 96.012 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "2e288686-9b4b-4d16-be1c-eaa26ed61eeb")
	)
	(text "The interrupt pin is an output or an \ninput (See your datasheet!) which \nsends a digital signal according to \ncertain conditions that are met in the \nsensor (again, reference a datasheet!).\nThis is usually when the sensor is \ndone sending data, or if something \nunexpected happens like signal lost."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 97.028 139.954 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "3a748fd6-cde6-41ad-90b0-a47449548276")
	)
	(text "VDD is often 3V3 (3.3 volts input).\nYou should however consult your\nsensor's datasheet to determine the\nrequired voltage."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 146.812 70.866 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "72faac1c-b0c1-44d6-abb0-ae530a6c6fd1")
	)
	(text "VDDIO simply refers to the voltage \nthat data is being streamed at. If\nyou send data from a microcontroller\nthat operates at 5V, the data is also\nprobably going to be 5V. But, as \nalways, make sure to read the \ndatasheet to verify what the logic\nvoltage is. If you send a 5V signal\nwhile the sensor expects 3.3V, you\ncould fry and break sommething."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 109.982 73.914 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "a1750fe3-98bf-42c2-bce5-6289d28787a6")
	)
	(text "Note 1:\nUsing SPI, you can actually hook up many different sensors to the same SPI line! The only added wires you'd need are \nunique CS lines. For each sensor, they each need their own CS wire. However, each sensor can be connected to the same\nMOSI, MISO, and CLK lines."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 13.208 190.5 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.905 1.905)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "b42bd56a-96a9-47a9-bf48-37993a5be3af")
	)
	(text "MISO (Master In Slave Out). \nThis corresponds to SDO (Sensor \nData Out), but on a datasheet \nit can often be something else. \nMake sure this is a data output \nfrom the sensor."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 59.182 69.596 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "b6503655-219c-45f8-82a7-863971460939")
	)
	(text "The CS pin is Chip Select. It \nturns on and off the sensor, \npreventing data output when\nyou don't need to. This is very\nuseful when you have multiple \nsensors connected to the same \nSPI data lines (See Note 1)."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 97.79 113.03 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "f0880c8a-69b7-4cb8-b938-91c66224e453")
	)
	(text "For the vast majority of the time,\nyou won't need to have two ground \nsources for data and power. If you\ndo, first ask, \"why is this necessary?\""
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 148.082 122.174 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "f1f7dd5a-d343-4573-bff1-79178e495d00")
	)
